---
<BlogLayout
  title="How to Choose the Right Bra Manufacturer for Your Brand"
  description="A practical guide for DTC brands looking for a reliable bra OEM partner — from MOQ considerations to quality certification requirements."
>
  <article class="container-site py-12 max-w-3xl">
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>
      <a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>
      <span class="text-navy font-medium">Choosing a Bra Manufacturer</span>
    </nav>

    <time class="text-sm text-slate">June 1, 2026</time>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy mt-2 mb-8">How to Choose the Right Bra Manufacturer for Your Brand</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-stone max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-stone leading-relaxed">
        Finding the right manufacturing partner is one of the most critical decisions for any intimate apparel brand. 
        The wrong choice can lead to quality issues, delayed shipments, and damaged brand reputation. 
        Here's a practical framework for evaluating bra manufacturers.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Start with Certification</h2>
      <p>Any serious bra manufacturer should hold at minimum BSCI (social compliance) and OEKO-TEX Standard 100 (fabric safety) certifications. These aren't just badges — they represent factory conditions, worker welfare, and material safety that directly impact your brand's liability and reputation.</p>

      <h2>2. Evaluate MOQ Fit</h2>
      <p>Most established bra manufacturers set MOQs between 200-500 pieces per design. If smaller quantities are needed, look for partners with a sample/small batch channel — this allows you to validate quality before scaling.</p>

      <h2>3. Check Category Specialization</h2>
      <p>Not all lingerie factories are equal. Some specialize in wirefree bras using seamless technology, others excel at full-cup underwire constructions. Ensure your factory's core competency matches your product line.</p>

      <h2>4. Request Samples Before Committing</h2>
      <p>Always request samples before placing bulk orders. Evaluate stitching consistency, fabric hand-feel, hardware quality (adjusters, hooks), and sizing accuracy across the graded range.</p>

      <h2>5. Communication & Lead Time</h2>
      <p>The best factory partners respond to technical questions within 24 hours, provide transparent production timelines, and proactively flag potential delays. Typical lead times: 7-10 days for samples, 30-45 days for bulk.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-cream border-l-4 border-accent">
      <p class="text-sm text-navy font-semibold">Looking for a bra manufacturer?</p>
